                            A m i N e t X D u o

              A TCP/IP stack for AmigaOS, on Eclipse ThreadX NetX Duo


WHAT IT IS
----------

AmiNetXDuo gives AmigaOS the bsdsocket.library that every Amiga networking
program already speaks.  It reaches the network card through the SANA-II
driver that every Amiga network card already has.  It is a complete stack,
not a shim: DHCP, ARP, ICMP, TCP, UDP and DNS.  The full stack adds IPv6,
with neighbour discovery, duplicate address detection, stateless
autoconfiguration and DHCPv6.

It is MIT licensed, with the exceptions listed under LICENCE.

The manual is Docs/AmiNetXDuo.guide.  This file is the short version.


BEFORE THE INSTALLATION
-----------------------

This machine needs:

  *  AmigaOS 2.04 (Kickstart 37) or newer.
  *  Any 68000 or better.  One binary serves every processor: the library
     selects the code for the machine when it opens.  Encrypted (https:)
     connections work on every processor, the 68000 included, but a first
     handshake there takes a minute or two.  A resumed one takes under a
     second.
  *  1 MB of memory.  The archive holds three stacks, and the installer
     asks which one to put on:

        Stack     Leaves out                                    RAM while up
        Full      nothing                                          426 KB
        Minimal   IPv6, .local lookups, the packet filter,
                  encryption, IPv4 multicast, the ARexx host,
                  the TCP: handler                                 256 KB
        Micro     all of that, and carries one interface and
                  no route or address-allocation calls.  DHCP
                  works                                            212 KB

     The stack sizes its packet buffers from what is left, so more memory
     means more buffers.  Encryption adds about 210 KB the first time it is
     used.
  *  A network card, and the SANA-II driver for that card.  The driver is a
     file whose name ends in ".device", and it came with the card.  Put the
     driver in DEVS: before the installation.  The installer then finds it
     and pre-selects the card.

The installer offers these cards by name, and writes the driver each one
uses:

     Commodore A2065 . . . . . . . . . a2065.device
     Village Tronic Ariadne  . . . . . ariadne.device
     Village Tronic Ariadne II . . . . ariadne_ii.device
     Hydra Systems AmigaNet  . . . . . hydra.device
     ASDG LAN Rover  . . . . . . . . . eb920.device
     Individual Computers X-Surf . . . x-surf.device
     Individual Computers X-Surf 100 . x-surf-100.device
     PCMCIA NE2000 or CNet . . . . . . cnet.device
     PCMCIA 3Com EtherLink III 3C589 . etherlink3.device
     PCMCIA Prism2 wireless  . . . . . prism2.device
     UAE (emulators) . . . . . . . . . uaenet.device

Any other SANA-II driver works too.  The last menu entry of the installer
takes the name of the driver.

The archive also carries anxnet.device, the SANA-II driver AmiNetXDuo builds
itself.  It drives the A2065, the Ariadne, the Ariadne II, the AmigaNet, the
LAN Rover, the X-Surf, the X-Surf 100, NE2000-compatible PCMCIA cards and
the 3C589.  See ANXNET.DEVICE below.


INSTALLING
----------

Double-click "Install-AmiNetXDuo".  Commodore's Installer is in the same
drawer, so nothing has to be fetched first.

From a Shell, the equivalent is:

     Stack 10000
     CD <the drawer this file is in>
     Installer SCRIPT Install-AmiNetXDuo APPNAME AmiNetXDuo

(Separate the keyword and the value with a space, not with "=".  Installer
parses its command line by hand and does not recognise "SCRIPT=name".  The
CD matters too: started from a Shell, the script finds its files relative to
the current directory.)

The installer asks, in this order:

     Which stack: Everything, Minimal or Micro   (Everything)
     Install anxnet.device                        (yes)
     Where the AmiNetXDuo drawer of documentation
       and examples goes                          (any drawer)
     Into the system, or into its own drawer      (into the system;
                                                   Intermediate and Expert)
     Keep an existing configuration               (yes, when there is one)
     Which card, and for PCMCIA which card type
     The interface name and unit                  (eth0, 0; Expert)
     Answer to <hostname>.local on the network    (no)
     DHCP, or a fixed address, netmask, gateway   (DHCP)
     The name of this machine                     (amiga- and the last
                                                   three octets of the
                                                   card's address)
     Start the network at boot                    (yes)
     Start httpd at boot, with a browser file
       manager and Shell; which drawer and port   (no)

At Novice level every question takes its default without being shown.

If the answer to the DHCP question is not known, answer yes.  Nearly every
home network hands out addresses, and the change afterwards is one line in
one file.

It puts

     bsdsocket.library, usergroup.library,
     tls.library                               into LIBS:
     ActivateAmiNetXDuo, NetSetup,
     AddNetInterface, RemoveNetInterface,
     ConfigureNetInterface, Online, Offline,
     ShowNetStatus, ShowNetServices, GetNetStatus,
     CheckNetConfig, CheckNetDevice, NetShutdown,
     AddNetRoute, DeleteNetRoute, hostname,
     ping, netstat, host, nslookup, arp,
     fetch, nc, telnet, tftp, sntp, traceroute,
     whois, ssh, scp, scp-runner, httpd, iperf,
     NetCapture, NetTrace                      into C:
     anxnet.device                             into DEVS:Networks
     the interface definition                  into DEVS:NetInterfaces
     the routing and name server files         into DEVS:Internet
     this file, the manual, the examples       into the selected drawer

and, when it is allowed to, S:Network-Startup, the file that starts the
network, plus a managed block in S:User-Startup that runs it.

The minimal and micro stacks have no tls.library and no
DEVS:Internet/certificates.  scp-runner is scp's helper and is not a command
to type.

At Intermediate or Expert level the stack can instead be installed entirely
inside the selected AmiNetXDuo drawer.  No existing system library, command,
driver, configuration file or AmiTCP: assign is changed.  At boot,
ActivateAmiNetXDuo puts the private Libs, C and Devs first while retaining
every directory already present in those assigns.  This is a boot-time
choice: disable AmiNetXDuo's startup block and enable the other stack's
block, then reboot, to switch back.  A bsdsocket.library already resident in
memory cannot be exchanged safely.

In the system layout, a bsdsocket.library already in LIBS: -- from AmiTCP,
Miami, Genesis or Roadshow -- is renamed to bsdsocket.library.old rather
than deleted, and the installer says so.  Every library is copied in under
a .new name first and swapped after, so a copy that fails leaves the
installed file in place.

The Developer drawer is not installed, deliberately: it holds headers, and
where headers belong is a property of the compiler rather than of this
machine.

A second run of the installer over a working installation is safe.  It
notices the existing configuration and offers to leave it exactly as it is,
and it only ever replaces its own block in S:User-Startup.  An existing
S:Network-Startup is renamed to S:Network-Startup.old.


HOW THE NETWORK STARTS
----------------------

A file in DEVS:NetInterfaces defines an interface.  It does not start it.
S:Network-Startup does, with one line per interface:

     C:AddNetInterface DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0 QUIET

S:User-Startup does nothing but Execute that file.  To add a card, to stop
the network coming up at boot, or to start every definition in the drawer
with

     C:AddNetInterface DEVS:NetInterfaces/~(#?.info) QUIET

edit S:Network-Startup.  An installation from 0.26.x or earlier started its
interfaces when the library opened; after an upgrade, that one line has to
be there.

Opening the library on its own gives a loopback-only stack: 127.0.0.1 and,
in the full stack, ::1 work at once, and no network driver is opened until
AddNetInterface or Online names one.


DID IT WORK?
------------

     ShowNetStatus              is it up, and what address did it get
     ping 192.168.1.1           can it reach the router
     host aminet.net            can it look a name up
     netstat -r                 what does it think the routes are

If ShowNetStatus reports that nothing is running, the usual reason is that
S:Network-Startup names no interface, or that the driver of the card is not
in DEVS:.  The next most usual reason is the wrong card chosen in the
installer.  Run the installer again and select another card.


DOING IT BY HAND
----------------

Everything the installer writes is a plain text file that can be written by
hand.  There is a commented example of each of them in the Examples drawer:

     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0        every keyword there is, explained
     Examples/NetInterfaces/a2065       the short version, one per card
     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0-static a fixed address, worked through
     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0-ipv6   IPv6 keywords, worked through
     Examples/Internet/routes           the default route
     Examples/Internet/name_resolution  name servers and the host name
     Examples/Internet/hosts            names known without a name server
     Examples/Internet/service_discovery  what this machine announces

The minimum that works is three lines in DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0:

     DEVICE=a2065.device
     UNIT=0
     CONFIGURE=DHCP

and the AddNetInterface line for it in S:Network-Startup.

Note CONFIGURE, not IPTYPE.  Documentation elsewhere on the net that says
IPTYPE=DHCP is repeating an AmiTCP-era mistake: in Roadshow IPTYPE is a
number, the SANA-II packet type.  AmiNetXDuo accepts both, so such a file
still works here, but it does not work on Roadshow.


ANXNET.DEVICE
-------------

Devs/Networks holds anxnet.device, AmiNetXDuo's own SANA-II driver, which
replaces the driver that came with the card.  The installer asks whether to
put it in DEVS:Networks and offers yes.  Copying it changes nothing on its
own, because nothing reads it until an interface file names it.  To use it,
put DEVICE=anxnet.device in DEVS:NetInterfaces/<name>, and add a CARD= line
when the probe has to be told which board it is.  CheckNetDevice reports
what it found, card by card, and why any card was refused.  An anxnet.device
already on the machine is renamed to anxnet.device.old rather than being
overwritten.


USING SSH
---------

`ssh' is Dropbear's dbclient and `scp' is Dropbear's scp.  Both want
mathieeedoubbas.library in LIBS:, which every Workbench install has, and
nothing else.  They bring their own stack, so there is no `stack 200000' to
type first.

     ssh me@192.168.1.10                    log in
     ssh me@192.168.1.10 "uname -a"         run one command
     scp Work:file me@192.168.1.10:         copy a file there
     scp -r me@192.168.1.10:dir Work:       copy a directory here

A password works if the far end allows one.  A key is better, and on a
machine this slow it also saves the typing.

THE KEY MUST BE IN DROPBEAR'S FORMAT, not OpenSSH's.  An OpenSSH key copied
straight over is not read.  On the PC, with Dropbear's own tools:

     dropbearkey -t ed25519 -f id_amiga     make one
     dropbearconvert openssh dropbear ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 id_amiga
                                            or convert one you already have
     dropbearkey -y -f id_amiga             print the line for the server's
                                            authorized_keys

MAKE THE KEY ON THE PC, not on the Amiga.  This machine has very little
entropy to make one from, and ed25519 keygen on a 14 MHz 68020 takes about a
minute.  Copy the private key over afterwards.

NAME THE KEY WITH -i, every time:

     ssh -i DH0:keys/id_amiga me@192.168.1.10

There is a default path -- dbclient reads ENV:HOME, appends /.ssh/id_dropbear,
and falls back to SYS: when ENV:HOME is unset -- but a key left there with
-i omitted is not found; the connection is refused with "No auth methods
could be used".  Use -i and it works every time.

ENV:USER is the name sent when the command line has no user@ in front of the
host; with none set it is "amiga".

     SetEnv SAVE USER me


WRITING PROGRAMS AGAINST IT
---------------------------

The Developer drawer holds the headers for what bsdsocket.library has that
the NDK does not declare: the RFC 3493 interface calls, if_nametoindex() and
its three companions, and the AF_INET6 names, from IPPROTO_IPV6 and
IPV6_V6ONLY to the IN6_IS_ADDR_* macros.  It also holds the SDK for
tls.library, which runs on any bsdsocket.library.  The installer does not
copy the drawer, because the location of headers is a property of the
compiler rather than of the machine.  Put it wherever the NDK is.
Developer/ReadMe explains the rest, and Developer/examples holds programs
that work.

Nothing else needs it.  Everything the NDK already declares is reached the
way it always was.


UNINSTALLING
------------

     Delete LIBS:bsdsocket.library LIBS:usergroup.library
     Delete LIBS:tls.library
     Delete C:ActivateAmiNetXDuo C:NetSetup
     Delete C:AddNetInterface C:RemoveNetInterface
     Delete C:ConfigureNetInterface C:Online C:Offline
     Delete C:ShowNetStatus C:ShowNetServices C:GetNetStatus
     Delete C:CheckNetConfig C:CheckNetDevice C:NetShutdown
     Delete C:AddNetRoute C:DeleteNetRoute C:hostname
     Delete C:ping C:netstat C:host C:nslookup C:arp
     Delete C:fetch C:nc C:telnet C:tftp C:sntp C:traceroute
     Delete C:whois C:ssh C:scp C:scp-runner C:httpd C:iperf
     Delete C:NetCapture C:NetTrace
     Delete DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0
     Delete DEVS:Internet/routes DEVS:Internet/name_resolution
     Delete DEVS:Internet/hosts DEVS:Internet/certificates
     Delete DEVS:Networks/anxnet.device
     Delete DEVS:Networks/anxnet.device.old
     Delete S:Network-Startup S:Network-Startup.old
     Delete <the AmiNetXDuo drawer> ALL

and take the ";BEGIN AmiNetXDuo" .. ";END AmiNetXDuo" block out of
S:User-Startup.  If another stack was installed before, rename
LIBS:bsdsocket.library.old back over LIBS:bsdsocket.library.  Add QUIET to
a Delete line whose file was never installed, such as tls.library on a
minimal or micro installation.

A drawer installation is the AmiNetXDuo drawer, the startup block, and
nothing else: delete the drawer and take the block out.


STATUS
------

It gets a DHCP lease, answers ARP, pings its gateway, resolves DNS, moves
TCP in both directions and listens for incoming connections.
bsdsocket.library scores 142 of 142 on the bsdsocktest conformance suite.

Everything above was measured on an emulated 68000, 68020 and 68030 under
Kickstart 2.04 and 3.1, and on real hardware: an A1200 with a 3C589, and an
A3000 with an X-Surf-100, where a user measured 795 KB/s.


